WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:03.700
There's no way Hugh Jackman could have known
he would wind up playing Wolverine in nine

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.950
films over nearly 20 years.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.320
Especially since he only got the part as a
last-minute replacement.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.000
A relative unknown at the time of his casting,
Jackman has used the part to launch a blockbuster

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.819
career, bringing legendary musicals to the
screen, hosting Oscar telecasts, and becoming

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.480
one of the most beloved faces Hollywood has
seen in decades.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:29.300
"I'm Wolveriiiiine"

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.660
Now that he's delivered his X-Men swan song,
let's take a look back at the highs and lows

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.580
of his time as Wolverine and rank what worked—and
what made us go berserk.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.640
X-Men Origins: Wolverine

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.720
There are bad movies, and then there's whatever
happened to X-Men Origins: Wolverine.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.180
For starters, the movie just wasn't necessary.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.560
Sure, the plot arc with Sabretooth was interesting,
but we didn't need a boxing match between

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:54.820
Logan and the Blob, and Logan's adamantium
operation had already been covered well enough

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.789
in X2: X-Men United.

00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:01.400
On top of all that, X-Men Origins featured
the some of the worst CG in the entire franchise.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.260
Wait, can we see those claws again?

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.220
"Ooh, shiny."

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.740
But more than anything, the sloppy writing
derailed any chance at redemption.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:16.140
Not only did Wolverine have to walk away from
an explosion in slo-mo with a straight face,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.080
but he also had to scream into the air not
once, but twice.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.260
"Ah."

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.360
Here's a movie that introduced Ryan Reynolds
as Deadpool, then sewed his mouth shut, just

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.060
to make sure there was no chance he could
be remotely enjoyable.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.560
"That's just lazy writing."

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.120
Jackman did his best with the corny screenplay,
but this movie is somehow a parody of itself

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.409
without ever figuring out how to make that
fun.

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:42.380
That's a feat, but not one worth watching.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.340
X-Men: The Last Stand

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.320
The conclusion to the original X-Men trilogy
is so bad and so overstuffed, it almost ended

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.579
a franchise even Magneto couldn't bring down.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.079
With Bryan Singer departing at the last minute
to helm Superman Returns, directing duties

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.290
for the third X-Men movie fell to Brett Ratner.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.780
At best this movie is forgettable, at worst
it's a tutorial on how not to make a blockbuster.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.590
If you can think of a character from the X-Men
universe, they probably show up here at some

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.700
point, spouting some awful catchphrase before
fading back into the special effects.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.240
Or worse: Cyclops, who had been central to
the first two movies, is killed off early

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.640
and then never mentioned again.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.820
Wolverine shows up, of course, but ends up
coming across as oddly neutered and bland.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.340
"Grow those back."

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.610
And then there's the "Dark Phoenix" storyline,
a pivotal arc from the X-Men comics that gets

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.180
tossed into the plot with about seven other
stories.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.580
Jackman did his best with the material, but
there was no saving this mutant monstrosity.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.520
X-Men: First Class

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.900
He may not have had a part in the main story,
but Wolverine was definitely memorable in

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.940
X-Men: First Class, showing up to deliver
one line before disappearing for the rest

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:50.700
of the movie.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.420
"Excuse me, I'm Eric Lechurr."

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:52.920
"I'm Charles Xavier."

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:53.740
"Go F--- yourself."

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.560
It may be short, even by cameo standards,
but it also may be the funniest scene Jackman

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.760
ever got to film as the surly superhero.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.160
Although we can only imagine what he would
have said about those yellow jumpsuits.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:08.740
"Do we really have to wear these?"

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.640
X-Men: Apocalypse

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.420
Unlike his brief cameo in First Class, Jackman's
appearance in X-Men: Apocalypse finally lets

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:17.700
Wolverine cut loose.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.700
The Wolverine from the comic books has always
nurtured a darker side, which shows itself

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.960
in the peak of battle as his "berserker rage."

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.000
The mainstream movies shied away from this,
for the most part.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.460
Thankfully, eight movies in, they finally
decided to cut him loose.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.920
Back at the familiar setting of Alkali Lake,
which played a big part in Wolverine's origin

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.420
story, we see Weapon X as a caged animal,
finally getting a chance to fight for his

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:39.920
freedom.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.740
It's a moment fans of the comics have waited
decades to see.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.840
While the rest of the movie is sort of hectic,
Jackman's brief moment on the screen is a

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.960
revelation, and helped lay the groundwork
for what was to come.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.380
X-Men: Days of Future Past

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:56.540
Wolverine can be such a tortured character
that it's all the more fun when he gets the

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.440
chance to just loosen up for a minute.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.140
Trying to save the world seems like an odd
time to crack a smile, but while the stakes

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:05.730
are high, there's no doubt the tone in this
film is lighter than some of Wolvie's other

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:06.230
outings.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.460
"Would you believe me if I told you I was
sent here from the future,"

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:16.060
Sure, fans of the comic can debate the decision
to swap out Kitty Pryde, who did all the time

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:19.829
traveling in the comic book storyline, but
sending Wolverine back in time instead was

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.260
a smart way to bridge the original and rebooted
X-Men casts.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:24.820
X-Men

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.260
This is where it all started, and no one knew
if it could work.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.600
What would a full-blown superhero team, full
of wacky powers, look like on the big screen?

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.260
Would they wear fluorescent spandex?

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.860
"You actually go outside in these things…?"

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.240
"What would you prefer.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:40.920
Yellow spandex?"

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.440
More importantly, would audiences turn out
for comic book characters that weren't Superman,

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:47.460
Batman or Spider-Man?

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.220
With X-Men, those questions were definitively
answered.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.400
It was faithful to the comics it adapted,
but used just enough black leather to welcome

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.200
mainstream audiences into the fold.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.280
And no one helped carry the film, or captured
the imagination of the audience more, than

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.200
Jackman with his portrayal of Wolverine.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.770
An amnesiatic drifter without a past or a
future, Wolvie was the audience's window into

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:08.759
this world of outcast and misfits.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.440
If we found them weird, that was okay — because
he did too.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:15.780
Jackman became a star the moment he appeared
on screen, and Wolverine proved he could brawl

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.760
with the best of them, spandex and all.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.520
The Wolverine

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:24.300
Following the undeniable failure of X-Men
Origins: Wolverine, acclaimed director James

00:05:24.319 --> 00:05:27.650
Mangold was brought in to clean up the Wolverine
franchise.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.919
And for two-thirds of The Wolverine he did
just that, crafting a nuanced, intimate look

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:36.419
at Logan and drawing heavily on perhaps his
most celebrated comic book plotline: Chris

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.180
Claremont and Frank Miller's Wolverine limited
series.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:43.010
By rooting the movie in his grief at the loss
of Jean Grey, and temporarily taking away

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:46.249
his healing power, this movie shows Wolverine
at his most vulnerable.

00:05:46.249 --> 00:05:50.569
True, the third act then descended into the
typical bloat of a big-budget action movie,

00:05:50.569 --> 00:05:53.300
with cartoonish robots and CGI-heavy fight
scenes.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.380
But it showed that there were more Logan stories
to tell, especially if you stick to his source

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.220
material as closely as possible.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.580
X2: X-Men United

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.879
The best standalone X-Men movie is without
a doubt X2: X-Men United, thanks largely to

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.320
its Wolverine-centered plot.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:12.889
The franchise took this step forward by looking
to its past, specifically the mysterious background

00:06:12.889 --> 00:06:14.340
of its most tortured hero.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.139
By pitting Logan against William Stryker,
the man who coated Logan's bones with adamantium

00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:23.560
and turned his memory into Swiss cheese, we
got to see a plot that was both personal and

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:24.240
explosive.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.380
And its central set piece is perhaps the best
blend of action and emotion this franchise

00:06:28.389 --> 00:06:32.449
has ever delivered, when babysitter Logan
hints at his beserker rage for the first time

00:06:32.449 --> 00:06:32.949
onscreen.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:33.700
"Babysitter?"

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.759
While the first X-Men movie set the stage,
this is what elevated the franchise into an

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:39.720
out-and-out hit.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:41.420
Logan

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.740
It only took nine tries to get it perfect…but
boy, was it worth the wait.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.659
This is the Wolverine we've all been waiting
for, a grizzled bastard who isn't looking

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:52.800
for a fight, but will definitely finish one
if it starts.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.539
Loosely inspired by the acclaimed and influential
Old Man Logan arc, it tells the story of an

00:06:57.539 --> 00:07:01.710
aged, grieving veteran of too many superhero
battles who wants to disappear—but gets

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.320
pulled back in one last time.

00:07:03.509 --> 00:07:07.620
With a hard R rating that allows the blood
and guts Wolverine is famous for, nobody holds

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:08.240
back here.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.300
Not the filmmakers, and not our hero.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.720
Like a Johnny Cash song come to life, Logan
is both poetic and gritty — and it's one

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.640
of the best superhero movies ever made.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:18.620
What's next?

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.920
Now that Jackman's Wolverine has been laid
to rest, fans have been speculating about

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.340
where the character will go next.

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:28.980
Considering Daphne Keen's show-stopping performance
as Laura in Logan, it's possible that she'll

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:33.340
become the next on-screen Wolverine — especially
since the same thing has happened in the comics.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:38.020
But the comics also delve into a range of
other scenarios, from alternate universe Wolverines

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.060
to Logan himself coming back from the grave.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.240
In other words, these are comic book movies.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:43.100
Anything's possible.

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:47.440
As far as Jackman's concerned, he doesn't
particularly care who takes up the adamantium

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.280
mantle...with one exception.

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:53.680
As he jokingly told the New York Times, anyone
could become the new Wolverine, "unless Daniel

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.009
Day-Lewis plays him and wins the Oscar — then
I might have a little problem."

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:01.089
With the Fox-Disney merger on the horizon,
only time will tell what the future has in

00:08:01.089 --> 00:08:02.420
store for Wolverine.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:03.480
Thanks for watching!

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.340
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:06.349 --> 00:08:08.659
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

